Chapter: GopAcpi
  cites Works chapter GopHandoff

 ACPI table discovery for the Option A boot path (H1b). Until now the
 payload assumed the LAPIC at 0xFEE00000 and the IOAPIC at 0xFEC00000
 and had no idea how to power the machine off -- a hardcoded gamble
 that happens to hold on the boards we have tried. The firmware knows
 the real answers and publishes them in the ACPI tables; the boot stub
 captures the RSDP pointer from the UEFI configuration table (the RSDP
 is a vendor table, not a protocol, so LocateProtocol cannot find it)
 and leaves it in a cell. Everything here reads from that pointer.

 The whole chapter is pure. peek-byte, peek-32, and peek-qword carry no
 effect row, so the parser is an ordinary function over an address --
 which means it is testable against a table set built from the spec in
 a buffer, with no firmware and no device. codex/test/apps/acpi-parse
 does exactly that.

 Two shapes must both work. An ACPI 1.0 RSDP (revision 0) offers only a
 32-bit RsdtAddress, and the RSDT's entries are 32-bit. An ACPI 2.0+
 RSDP (revision 2 and up) also offers a 64-bit XsdtAddress whose entries
 are 64-bit; we prefer it. codex-vm publishes both. Real firmware on any
 machine made this century publishes both.

 We say:

Section: Little-Endian Reads

 Assembled from bytes rather than taken from peek-32, because a table
 field like the local APIC address (0xFEE00000) has bit 31 set and a
 sign-extending 32-bit load would report it as a negative number.

  acpi-rd16 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  acpi-rd16 (a) (o) = peek-byte a o + peek-byte a (o + 1) * 256

  acpi-rd32 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  acpi-rd32 (a) (o) =
    peek-byte a o
      + peek-byte a (o + 1) * 256
      + peek-byte a (o + 2) * 65536
      + peek-byte a (o + 3) * 16777216

  acpi-rd64 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  acpi-rd64 (a) (o) = acpi-rd32 a o + acpi-rd32 a (o + 4) * 4294967296

Section: Signatures and Checksums

 A conforming table's bytes sum to zero modulo 256. We verify the RSDP
 and the root table, whose lengths are small and known. We do not
 checksum the DSDT: on real firmware it runs to kilobytes of AML, and a
 bad byte there would surface as a failed _S5_ decode, which we handle.

  acpi-sum : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  acpi-sum (a) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc else acpi-sum a (i + 1) n (acc + peek-byte a i)

  acpi-checksum-ok : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  acpi-checksum-ok (a) (n) = int-mod (acpi-sum a 0 n 0) 256 == 0

  acpi-sig-ok : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  acpi-sig-ok (a) (b0) (b1) (b2) (b3) =
    peek-byte a 0 == b0 & peek-byte a 1 == b1
      & peek-byte a 2 == b2 & peek-byte a 3 == b3

 "RSD PTR " -- eight bytes, the trailing space included.

  acpi-rsdp-sig-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  acpi-rsdp-sig-ok (a) =
    acpi-sig-ok a 82 83 68 32
      & peek-byte a 4 == 80 & peek-byte a 5 == 84
      & peek-byte a 6 == 82 & peek-byte a 7 == 32

  acpi-table-len : Integer -> Integer
  acpi-table-len (t) = acpi-rd32 t 4

Section: Root Table Walk

 The RSDT holds 32-bit pointers from offset 36; the XSDT holds 64-bit
 pointers from the same offset. Entry count follows from the header
 length. acpi-find returns the address of the first table whose
 signature matches, or 0.

  acpi-entry-count : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  acpi-entry-count (root) (entsize) =
    let len = acpi-table-len root
    in if len <= 36 then 0 else (len - 36) / entsize

  acpi-entry-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  acpi-entry-at (root) (entsize) (i) =
    if entsize == 8 then acpi-rd64 root (36 + i * 8)
    else acpi-rd32 root (36 + i * 4)

  acpi-find : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  acpi-find (root) (entsize) (n) (i) (b0) (b1) (b2) (b3) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let t = acpi-entry-at root entsize i
    in if t /= 0 & acpi-sig-ok t b0 b1 b2 b3 then t
    else acpi-find root entsize n (i + 1) b0 b1 b2 b3

Section: MADT

 Interrupt controller structures start at offset 44, each a (type,
 length) pair. Type 1 is an I/O APIC, whose 32-bit address sits at
 offset 4 within the structure. Type 5 overrides the local APIC address
 with a 64-bit value and, when present, wins over the 32-bit field at
 MADT offset 36. A zero length would not advance the walk, so we stop.

  madt-lapic-base : Integer -> Integer
  madt-lapic-base (m) = acpi-rd32 m 36

  madt-scan : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  madt-scan (m) (off) (len) (want) =
    if off + 2 > len then 0
    else let t = peek-byte m off
    in let l = peek-byte m (off + 1)
    in if l == 0 then 0
    else if t == want then off
    else madt-scan m (off + l) len want

  madt-ioapic : Integer -> Integer
  madt-ioapic (m) =
    let off = madt-scan m 44 (acpi-table-len m) 1
    in if off == 0 then 0 else acpi-rd32 m (off + 4)

  madt-lapic-override : Integer -> Integer
  madt-lapic-override (m) =
    let off = madt-scan m 44 (acpi-table-len m) 5
    in if off == 0 then 0 else acpi-rd64 m (off + 4)

  madt-lapic : Integer -> Integer
  madt-lapic (m) =
    let ov = madt-lapic-override m
    in if ov /= 0 then ov else madt-lapic-base m

Section: FADT

 Field offsets are the spec's, and they are not obvious: FIRMWARE_CTRL
 at 36 and DSDT at 40, then the power-management block addresses --
 PM1a_EVT_BLK at 56 and PM1a_CNT_BLK at 64. Reading PM1a_CNT_BLK from
 the wrong offset yields the event block, and a shutdown written there
 does nothing. A revision 2 FADT (length past 148) carries a 64-bit
 X_DSDT at 140 that supersedes the 32-bit field.

  fadt-dsdt : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-dsdt (f) =
    let len = acpi-table-len f
    in if len >= 148 then
      let x = acpi-rd64 f 140
      in if x /= 0 then x else acpi-rd32 f 40
    else acpi-rd32 f 40

  fadt-pm1a-evt : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-pm1a-evt (f) = acpi-rd32 f 56

  fadt-pm1a-cnt : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-pm1a-cnt (f) = acpi-rd32 f 64

  fadt-pm1b-cnt : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-pm1b-cnt (f) = acpi-rd32 f 68

  fadt-pm1-cnt-len : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-pm1-cnt-len (f) = peek-byte f 89

  fadt-sci-int : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-sci-int (f) = acpi-rd16 f 46

 A revision 2 FADT (length 129 and up) may publish a reset register:
 flags bit 10 (RESET_REG_SUP) at offset 112 declares it, a Generic
 Address Structure at 116 names the register (address space id first,
 the 64-bit address at 120), and RESET_VALUE at 128 is the byte to
 write. A shorter FADT has no such fields and reading them would be
 reading the next table's bytes.

  fadt-flags : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-flags (f) = acpi-rd32 f 112

  fadt-reset-space : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-reset-space (f) = peek-byte f 116

  fadt-reset-addr : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-reset-addr (f) = acpi-rd64 f 120

  fadt-reset-value : Integer -> Integer
  fadt-reset-value (f) = peek-byte f 128

  fadt-reset-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  fadt-reset-ok (f) =
    if acpi-table-len f < 129 then False
    else if bit-and (fadt-flags f) 1024 == 0 then False
    else fadt-reset-addr f /= 0

Section: The _S5_ Sleep Package

 To power the machine off an OS writes the sleep type the firmware
 chose, not a number it guessed. That number lives in the DSDT as
 Name (_S5_, Package () { SLP_TYPa, SLP_TYPb, ... }), encoded in AML.
 This is a targeted scan, not an AML interpreter: find the four bytes
 "_S5_", require a PackageOp immediately after, skip the package length
 and the element count, and decode the first two elements.

 A package length's leading byte carries the count of following length
 bytes in its top two bits. Elements here are constants: ZeroOp (0x00)
 is 0, OneOp (0x01) is 1, and BytePrefix (0x0A) introduces a literal
 byte. Anything else means an encoding we do not read, and we decline.

  s5-name-at : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  s5-name-at (d) (p) =
    peek-byte d p == 95 & peek-byte d (p + 1) == 83
      & peek-byte d (p + 2) == 53 & peek-byte d (p + 3) == 95

  s5-search : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s5-search (d) (p) (limit) =
    if p >= limit then (-1)
    else if s5-name-at d p & peek-byte d (p + 4) == 18 then p
    else s5-search d (p + 1) limit

 The element cursor: past "_S5_" (4) and PackageOp (1) sits the package
 length byte, then that many extra length bytes, then NumElements.

  s5-elems-start : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s5-elems-start (d) (p) =
    let pl = peek-byte d (p + 5)
    in let extra = bit-shru pl 6
    in p + 5 + 1 + extra + 1

  s5-elem-size : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s5-elem-size (d) (e) = if peek-byte d e == 10 then 2 else 1

  s5-elem-value : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s5-elem-value (d) (e) =
    let b = peek-byte d e
    in if b == 0 then 0 else if b == 1 then 1
    else if b == 10 then peek-byte d (e + 1) else (-1)

  s5-typa : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s5-typa (d) (p) = s5-elem-value d (s5-elems-start d p)

  s5-typb : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s5-typb (d) (p) =
    let e0 = s5-elems-start d p
    in s5-elem-value d (e0 + s5-elem-size d e0)

Section: The Parse

  AcpiInfo = record {
    ai-ok : Boolean,
    ai-rsdp : Integer,
    ai-revision : Integer,
    ai-root : Integer,
    ai-xsdt : Boolean,
    ai-tables : Integer,
    ai-madt : Integer,
    ai-fadt : Integer,
    ai-dsdt : Integer,
    ai-lapic : Integer,
    ai-ioapic : Integer,
    ai-pm1a-evt : Integer,
    ai-pm1a-cnt : Integer,
    ai-pm1b-cnt : Integer,
    ai-pm1-cnt-len : Integer,
    ai-sci : Integer,
    ai-s5-ok : Boolean,
    ai-slp-typa : Integer,
    ai-slp-typb : Integer,
    ai-reset-ok : Boolean,
    ai-reset-space : Integer,
    ai-reset-addr : Integer,
    ai-reset-value : Integer
  }

  acpi-none : Integer -> AcpiInfo
  acpi-none (rsdp) = AcpiInfo {
    ai-ok = False, ai-rsdp = rsdp, ai-revision = 0, ai-root = 0,
    ai-xsdt = False, ai-tables = 0, ai-madt = 0, ai-fadt = 0, ai-dsdt = 0,
    ai-lapic = 0, ai-ioapic = 0, ai-pm1a-evt = 0, ai-pm1a-cnt = 0,
    ai-pm1b-cnt = 0, ai-pm1-cnt-len = 0, ai-sci = 0,
    ai-s5-ok = False, ai-slp-typa = 0, ai-slp-typb = 0,
    ai-reset-ok = False, ai-reset-space = 0, ai-reset-addr = 0,
    ai-reset-value = 0
  }

 A revision 2 RSDP must satisfy both checksums: the first twenty bytes
 for the benefit of a 1.0 reader, and the whole declared length.

  acpi-rsdp-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  acpi-rsdp-ok (r) =
    if acpi-rsdp-sig-ok r == False then False
    else if acpi-checksum-ok r 20 == False then False
    else if peek-byte r 15 < 2 then True
    else acpi-checksum-ok r (acpi-rd32 r 20)

  acpi-root-of : Integer -> Integer
  acpi-root-of (r) =
    if peek-byte r 15 >= 2 then
      let x = acpi-rd64 r 24
      in if x /= 0 then x else acpi-rd32 r 16
    else acpi-rd32 r 16

  acpi-entsize-of : Integer -> Integer
  acpi-entsize-of (root) = if acpi-sig-ok root 88 83 68 84 then 8 else 4

  acpi-parse : Integer -> AcpiInfo
  acpi-parse (rsdp) =
    if rsdp == 0 then acpi-none 0
    else if acpi-rsdp-ok rsdp == False then acpi-none rsdp
    else let root = acpi-root-of rsdp
    in if root == 0 then acpi-none rsdp
    else if acpi-checksum-ok root (acpi-table-len root) == False then acpi-none rsdp
    else acpi-from-root rsdp root

  acpi-from-root : Integer, Integer -> AcpiInfo
  acpi-from-root (rsdp) (root) =
    let entsize = acpi-entsize-of root
    in let n = acpi-entry-count root entsize
    in let madt = acpi-find root entsize n 0 65 80 73 67
    in let fadt = acpi-find root entsize n 0 70 65 67 80
    in acpi-assemble rsdp root entsize n madt fadt

  acpi-assemble : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> AcpiInfo
  acpi-assemble (rsdp) (root) (entsize) (n) (madt) (fadt) =
    let dsdt = if fadt == 0 then 0 else fadt-dsdt fadt
    in let s5 = if dsdt == 0 then (-1) else s5-search dsdt 36 (acpi-table-len dsdt - 8)
    in AcpiInfo {
      ai-ok = True,
      ai-rsdp = rsdp,
      ai-revision = peek-byte rsdp 15,
      ai-root = root,
      ai-xsdt = entsize == 8,
      ai-tables = n,
      ai-madt = madt,
      ai-fadt = fadt,
      ai-dsdt = dsdt,
      ai-lapic = if madt == 0 then 0 else madt-lapic madt,
      ai-ioapic = if madt == 0 then 0 else madt-ioapic madt,
      ai-pm1a-evt = if fadt == 0 then 0 else fadt-pm1a-evt fadt,
      ai-pm1a-cnt = if fadt == 0 then 0 else fadt-pm1a-cnt fadt,
      ai-pm1b-cnt = if fadt == 0 then 0 else fadt-pm1b-cnt fadt,
      ai-pm1-cnt-len = if fadt == 0 then 0 else fadt-pm1-cnt-len fadt,
      ai-sci = if fadt == 0 then 0 else fadt-sci-int fadt,
      ai-s5-ok = s5 >= 0 & (if s5 < 0 then False else s5-typa dsdt s5 >= 0),
      ai-slp-typa = if s5 < 0 then 0 else s5-typa dsdt s5,
      ai-slp-typb = if s5 < 0 then 0 else s5-typb dsdt s5,
      ai-reset-ok = if fadt == 0 then False else fadt-reset-ok fadt,
      ai-reset-space = if fadt == 0 then 0 else if fadt-reset-ok fadt then fadt-reset-space fadt else 0,
      ai-reset-addr = if fadt == 0 then 0 else if fadt-reset-ok fadt then fadt-reset-addr fadt else 0,
      ai-reset-value = if fadt == 0 then 0 else if fadt-reset-ok fadt then fadt-reset-value fadt else 0
    }

Section: Power Off

 The S5 transition: write the sleep type the firmware published, with
 SLP_EN set, to the PM1a control block. SLP_TYP occupies bits 10 to 12
 and SLP_EN is bit 13, so the value is (SLP_TYPa << 10) | 0x2000. When
 the FADT names a PM1b control block the same is done there with
 SLP_TYPb; a machine with a split control block needs both halves.

 We do not perform the legacy ACPI-enable handshake (write ACPI_ENABLE
 to SMI_CMD, then poll SCI_EN). UEFI firmware places the machine in
 ACPI mode before it hands control to a boot application, so the
 handshake belongs to the BIOS era, and issuing it blind would poke a
 port whose meaning we have not read. If a board ever needs it, the
 FADT already tells us its SMI_CMD and ACPI_ENABLE.

 acpi-poweroff refuses rather than guesses: without a parsed FADT, a
 decoded _S5_, and a control-block address, it returns -1 and the
 machine stays on. A caller that gets -1 has learned something true
 about the firmware.

  acpi-slp-value : Integer -> Integer
  acpi-slp-value (slp-typ) = slp-typ * 1024 + 8192

  acpi-can-poweroff : AcpiInfo -> Boolean
  acpi-can-poweroff (a) =
    a.ai-ok & a.ai-s5-ok & (a.ai-pm1a-cnt /= 0) & (a.ai-slp-typa >= 0)

  acpi-poweroff : AcpiInfo -> [Device.Port] Integer
  acpi-poweroff (a) = act
    if acpi-can-poweroff a == False then (-1)
    else act
      w1 <- port-out-16 (a.ai-pm1a-cnt) (acpi-slp-value (a.ai-slp-typa))
      w2 <- if a.ai-pm1b-cnt == 0 then 0
            else port-out-16 (a.ai-pm1b-cnt) (acpi-slp-value (a.ai-slp-typb))
      acpi-halt 0
    end
  end

 If the write lands, execution never reaches here. If the firmware
 declined the transition, spin rather than fall off the end of the
 world into whatever bytes follow.

  acpi-halt : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  acpi-halt (n) = acpi-halt (n + 1)

Section: Reset

 Warm reset, by preference order. The FADT's reset register is the
 firmware's own answer and is honored when declared: a byte written
 to an I/O port or a memory address, whichever the Generic Address
 Structure names. The 0xCF9 reset-control port is the universal PC
 chipset fallback (bit 1 selects a hard reset, bit 2 pulses it). The
 i8042 output-port pulse is last, and only when a status read shows a
 controller actually present -- 0xFF is the floating bus, and writing
 reset commands into it is theater. Unlike poweroff, reset never
 refuses: 0xCF9 exists on every PC-compatible chipset. If every write
 is somehow ignored, spin.

  acpi-reset : AcpiInfo -> [Device.Port] Integer
  acpi-reset (a) = act
    r <- if a.ai-reset-ok then acpi-reset-reg a else 0
    c1 <- port-out-byte 3321 2
    c2 <- port-out-byte 3321 6
    k <- port-in-byte 100
    p <- if k /= 255 then port-out-byte 100 254 else 0
    acpi-halt 0
  end

  acpi-reset-reg : AcpiInfo -> [Device.Port] Integer
  acpi-reset-reg (a) = act
    if a.ai-reset-space == 1 then port-out-byte (a.ai-reset-addr) (a.ai-reset-value)
    else if a.ai-reset-space == 0 then poke-byte (a.ai-reset-addr) 0 (a.ai-reset-value)
    else 0
  end

Section: Boot Cell

 The Option A stub writes the RSDP pointer it found in the UEFI
 configuration table to cell 0x8028, beside the framebuffer handoff.
 Zero means the firmware exposed no ACPI table at all.

  acpi-cell : Integer = 32768

  acpi-boot-rsdp : Integer -> Integer
  acpi-boot-rsdp (dummy) =
    if handoff-present 0 then peek-qword handoff-base 40
    else peek-qword acpi-cell 40

  acpi-boot : Integer -> AcpiInfo
  acpi-boot (dummy) = acpi-parse (acpi-boot-rsdp 0)
